const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between acrylic, polyurethane and silicone conformal coating?',
    a: 'Acrylic gives good moisture protection with the easiest rework and the most forgiving process, but limited chemical and abrasion resistance. Polyurethane trades reworkability for markedly better chemical, solvent and abrasion resistance, which is why it dominates automotive and fluid-exposed applications. Silicone covers the widest temperature range and stays flexible under thermal cycling and vibration, at the cost of poor abrasion resistance and awkward partial rework, since almost nothing bonds over cured silicone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which conformal coating is easiest to rework?',
    a: 'Acrylic, by a wide margin. It can be dissolved with appropriate solvents and in many cases soldered through directly, which makes it the default choice for assemblies expected to be repaired in the field. Polyurethane needs deliberate thermal, mechanical or chemical removal. Silicone can be removed, but any area to be re-coated must be cleared completely, because a new coating will not bond over cured silicone residue.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which conformal coating is best for high temperature?',
    a: 'Silicone. Its usable range extends well beyond what acrylic or polyurethane tolerate, and it stays flexible rather than embrittling, so it survives repeated thermal cycling and the differential expansion that cracks harder films at solder fillets. It is the usual choice for LED assemblies, power electronics and anything running hot continuously.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which conformal coating is best for automotive electronics?',
    a: 'Polyurethane is the common answer for under-bonnet and fluid-exposed automotive assemblies, because fuel, oil and cleaning chemicals are exactly what it resists best. Silicone is preferred where the dominant stress is high temperature or vibration rather than chemical attack. The Peters ELPEGUARD and ELPECAST ranges are approved by the automotive industry across both families.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a water-based conformal coating as protective as a solvent-based one?',
    a: 'For most industrial requirements, yes — the protection class is set by the cured polymer, not by what carried it onto the board. Water-based (aqua) systems cut VOC emissions and the ventilation and operator-exposure burden that comes with solvents. The trade is a longer dry-off stage and more sensitivity to board cleanliness, since water wets a contaminated surface less forgivingly than solvent does.',
  },
];
